] 17 taxmann.com 107/34 STT 306 held that storage and warehousing service could not have been rendered without creating a warehouse and, consequently, the credit on TMT bars used for construction of warehouse would be admissible. Similarly, in the instant case, the appellant is unable to render the repair/refit service without the crane in dry dock and which can be treated as capital goods. [Para 14]

Cenvat credit on basis of invoices issued by ISD There is a denial of Cenvat credit availed on input service on the ground that credit was taken on the strength of invoices issued by the appellant's head office at Mumbai as ISD (Input Service Distributor). It is observed by the adjudicating authority that the head office address was not mentioned in the LOP issued by the DGFT. There is no dispute that the head office is registered as ISD with the Service Tax authorities. As per Cenvat Credit Rules, the appellants are eligible to avail the credit on the invoices issued by the ISD. There is no reason to deny the credit on the ground that the address of the ISD was not mentioned in the LOP. [Para 15]

In view of the above discussion, the denial of Cenvat credit along with interest and penalties cannot be sustained. Accordingly, the impugned order is set aside. [Para 16]

facts.text:
Facts

The assessee was manufacturers of motor vehicles falling under Chapter 8703 availing Modvat Credit on inputs and capital goods.

The assessee adopted the following procedure -

assessee was buying inputs from POS for direct delivery to the job-worker JBM ;

on getting the information, assessee would first reverse the credit equivalent to the amount involved in the transaction of delivery between POS and JBM ;

on receipt of the input invoices from POS, assessee would proceed to avail credit (as against credit already reversed) ; and

after receipt of the inputs from POS, JBM would process the goods and pay duty and dispatch the same to the assessee ;

on receipt of the goods alongwith the duty paid invoices from JBM, assessee would proceed to avail credit.

The Department denied Cenvat Credit availed by assessee on ground that —

assessee had not received the inputs in their factory but same were supplied directly to the job workers premises,

they have availed credit in their books of accounts without receipt of the goods and subsequently raised invoices/challans to the job workers.

The Department also argued that the primary conditions for availing credit is payment of duty on the inputs, receipt of the goods in the factory and usage of the inputs in the manufacture of final products. The assessee had not fulfilled said basic conditions in toto. Further, the assessee has raised challans/invoices to facilitate availment of credit by the job workers without supplying the raw materials from their factory.

The assessee argued that -

entire exercise is revenue neutral ;

only allegation made in the show cause notices is that inputs not received in their factory but delivered directly to the job workers whereas, the department has brought additional grounds in their grounds of appeal, which is beyond the scope of show cause notices ;

they have issued challans to the job worker only after reversing the credit and the final credit was availed only on receipt of the goods from the job workers ;

the original duty paying documents issued by the principal supplier of the goods were in the name of assessee.

Issue Involved

Whether credit could be denied ?

Whether procedure employed by assessee was revenue neutral ?

